WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.880
Serving LLMs over APIs turns model inference into an operational service.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.025
The model becomes one component inside an architecture that includes clients, gateways,

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:14.290
backends, inference servers, runtimes, model files, tokenizers, compute resources, queues,

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.130
logs, monitoring, and storage.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.015
The service boundary is where reliability, security, privacy,

00:00:19.015 --> 00:00:20.710
and application behavior meet.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.140
Strong implementations combine clear architecture, suitable model and runtime choices,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.770
secure API design, controlled exposure, performance management, privacy-aware logging,

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.240
and operational guardrails.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.150
They document working configurations, define authentication and authorization,

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:38.690
limit request size and rate, validate structured output, monitor health,

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.160
and handle failure gracefully.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.345
Privacy and data handling are central because prompts and outputs can contain sensitive

00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:44.850
information.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.675
Logging, retention, analytics, prompt history, backups,

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:50.135
and debug traces should be designed deliberately.

00:00:50.135 --> 00:00:52.200
Local or private serving can improve control,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.875
but only if the surrounding service does not create new data leaks or access paths.

00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:59.190
The practical goal is reliable, observable, least-privileged,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.140
privacy-aware inference that applications can use safely.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.500
A model that responds is only the beginning.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.495
A production-quality LLM API makes access explicit, behavior measurable,

00:01:08.495 --> 00:01:10.850
failures understandable, data handling intentional,

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.680
and downstream use controlled enough for real systems.
